# Change Log
All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

## [2.0.0] - 2026-10-24
### Added
- Range steps: 'x-y/z' is every z'th value from x to y (ie: '1-10/3' is 1,
4, 7 and 10).
- libdateblock shared library (with headers and a pkg-config file) that the
command line tool and Python bindings both use.
- `make check` runs the unit tests and command line tests.
- Packaging for Fedora and RHEL 9/10 (RPM), Debian 13 (.deb) and Arch
Linux, all kept in packaging/.
- `make docker-build`, `make docker-rpm`, `make docker-deb`,
`make docker-arch` and friends build and test everything in a container.
- The man page now covers every option and is generated from
packaging/man/dateblock.md.
- `pip install dateblock` installs the Python library on Linux, macOS and
Windows (the `dateblock` command is the C++ tool). Wheels are built for
Python 3.9 to 3.14.
- `make docker-pypi` builds the PyPI files; `make release` builds every
package into dist/release/ with checksums.
- macOS and Windows support in the library (time and sleep functions).

### Changed
- The project has been renamed from datetools to dateblock.
- Python bindings now target Python 3 (Python 2 is no longer supported),
and live in a `dateblock` package (`from dateblock import dateblock` still
works).
- The project page on PyPI now renders its Markdown description.
- Python: an unsupported `ref` value raises `TypeError` (was `SyntaxError`),
and a cron that can never happen raises `ValueError` (instead of exiting
the interpreter).
- Python: blocking no longer holds the GIL, and Ctrl-C now interrupts it.
- Blocking wakes up exactly on the target second.
- A cron that can never happen (such as February 31st) is reported as an
error; impossible combinations (such as '30,31' of '2,3') are now skipped
instead of aborting.
- Boost is now only needed by the command line tool.
- A new matching engine works on whole field lists at once instead of
trying every combination one at a time, so long lists such as
'0-59 0-59 0-23' are as quick as single values.
- Fields that are not given only reset to their lowest value when they are
smaller than the smallest field given, as documented: '/5 * 3,4' is every
5th second during hours 3 and 4 (minutes are no longer forced to 0).
- --isc reads a crontab entry the way crontab(5) does: every field except
the seconds means what it says ('* 3 * * *' is every minute of hour 3), and
when both day fields are restricted either one may match. '*/x' counts
from the field's lowest value ('*/5' days are the 1st, 6th, 11th...).
- A schedule is reported as impossible when nothing matches within 400
years (one full calendar cycle).
- Unfinished entries such as '5-' are rejected instead of matching any
value, and a field may expand to at most 10,000 values.

### Removed
- The datemath tool. `date -d` (ie: `date -d '+3 days -2 hours'`) does the
same job.

### Fixed
- Date formatting now uses strftime(), fixing %I, %j, %P, %U, %x, %X and %h.
- Setting a reference date while today is the 31st no longer rolls over
into the following month.
- Month lengths are no longer off by a day around daylight saving changes.
- Rare schedules such as Feb 29th on a given weekday are found instead of
being reported as impossible.
- A month-only schedule (ie: '-m 3') starts on the 1st instead of keeping
today's day of the month.
- Setting a reference time on a daylight saving change day is no longer an
hour off.
- Drift values near 2147483647 no longer overflow into an endless loop.
- Python: a float `ref` of NaN, infinity or out of range raises
`OverflowError`, and an unexpected C++ error no longer aborts the
interpreter.

## [1.0.3] - 2018-05-31
### Changed
- fixed padding issue with dates (padding with a zero)
- pypi support

## [1.0.0] - 2017-04-21
### Added
- Drift option now supported inline as part of the cron
- using a plus (+) you can denote the drift entry in the cron. For example:
'0 /5 +4' is the same as writing '0 /5 * * * * 4'.  In this example we would
unblock every 5 minutes but drift an additional 4 seconds.  Inline drifting
allows multiple drifts such as '0 /5 +15,45' giving the same benifits as the
other fields do using this option.
- Internet Systems Consortium (ISC) support added (which is effectively
support for the standard cron format in addition to the enhanced one provided
by dateblock.

## [0.8.1] - 2015-08-17
### Changed
- Fixed bug where month was missing in printout formatting

## [0.8.0] - 2013-10-26
### Added
- Initial commit to GitHub
